#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:3
msgid "Making packages compatible"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:7
msgid "Developing for pipx"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:9
msgid "If you are a developer and want users to be able to run"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:11
msgid "$ pipx install MY_PACKAGE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:15
msgid ""
"make sure you include ``scripts`` in your main table [#main-table]_ in "
"``pyproject.toml`` or its legacy equivalents for ``setup.cfg`` and ``setup."
"py``. pipx also exposes ``gui-scripts`` entry points, which are useful for "
"GUI applications on Windows (they launch without opening a console window)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:0
msgid "pyproject.toml"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:23
msgid ""
"[project.scripts]\n"
"foo = \"my_package.some_module:main_func\"\n"
"bar = \"other_module:some_func\"\n"
"\n"
"[project.gui-scripts]\n"
"baz = \"my_package_gui:start_func\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:0
msgid "setup.cfg"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:34
msgid ""
"[options.entry_points]\n"
"console_scripts =\n"
"    foo = my_package.some_module:main_func\n"
"    bar = other_module:some_func\n"
"gui_scripts =\n"
"    baz = my_package_gui:start_func"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:0
msgid "setup.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:45
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    # other arguments here...\n"
"    entry_points={\n"
"        \"console_scripts\": [\n"
"            \"foo = my_package.some_module:main_func\",\n"
"            \"bar = other_module:some_func\",\n"
"        ],\n"
"        \"gui_scripts\": [\n"
"            \"baz = my_package_gui:start_func\",\n"
"        ],\n"
"    },\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:60
msgid ""
"Here ``foo`` and ``bar`` (and ``baz`` on Windows) become applications that "
"pipx exposes after installing the package, invoking their corresponding "
"entry point functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:83
msgid "The ``pipx.run`` entry point group"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:85
msgid ""
"When a user runs ``pipx run PACKAGE``, pipx looks for a console script "
"matching the package name. If the package name and script name differ, the "
"user has to write ``pipx run --spec PACKAGE SCRIPT``, which is less "
"convenient."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:88
msgid ""
"Package authors can declare a ``pipx.run`` entry point group to tell pipx "
"which function to invoke for ``pipx run``. This entry point takes priority "
"over console scripts when present."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:95
msgid ""
"[project.entry-points.\"pipx.run\"]\n"
"my-package = \"my_package.cli:main\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:102
msgid ""
"[options.entry_points]\n"
"pipx.run =\n"
"    my-package = my_package.cli:main"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:108
msgid ""
"With this declaration, ``pipx run my-package`` invokes ``my_package.cli:"
"main`` even if no console script named ``my-package`` exists. The `build "
"<https://github.com/pypa/build>`_ package uses this pattern so that ``pipx "
"run build`` works even though build's console script is named ``pyproject-"
"build``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:113
msgid "Detect a pipx installation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:115
msgid ""
"pipx records the environment name in ``pipx_metadata.json`` for packages "
"installed with ``pipx install``. Package code can read this name when it "
"needs to give pipx-specific recovery instructions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:118
msgid ""
"import json\n"
"import sys\n"
"from pathlib import Path\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def pipx_environment() -> str | None:\n"
"    try:\n"
"        metadata = json.loads((Path(sys.prefix) / \"pipx_metadata.json\")."
"read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n"
"    except (json.JSONDecodeError, OSError):\n"
"        return None\n"
"    environment = metadata.get(\"environment\") if isinstance(metadata, "
"dict) else None\n"
"    return environment if isinstance(environment, str) else None"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:133
msgid ""
"Use the returned name as the target for commands such as ``pipx inject "
"ENVIRONMENT 'mytool[feature]'``. pipx records the extra on the main package, "
"so reinstalling the environment retains it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:136
msgid ""
"``pipx run`` may create internal metadata without setting ``environment``, "
"so check the field value. Treat other keys as internal pipx state. If the "
"function returns ``None``, show the package's usual installer-neutral advice."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:140
msgid "Manual pages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:142
msgid ""
"To provide documentation via ``man`` pages on UNIX-like systems, ship them "
"as data files. pipx exposes them the same way it exposes apps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:0
msgid "setuptools"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:149
msgid ""
"# pyproject.toml\n"
"[tool.setuptools.data-files]\n"
"\"share/man/man1\" = [\n"
"  \"manpage.1\",\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:157
msgid ""
"# setup.cfg\n"
"[options.data_files]\n"
"share/man/man1 =\n"
"    manpage.1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:164
msgid ""
"# setup.py\n"
"setup(\n"
"    # other arguments here...\n"
"    data_files=[(\"share/man/man1\", [\"manpage.1\"])],\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:174
msgid ""
"The ``data-files`` keyword is \"discouraged\" in the `setuptools "
"documentation <https://setuptools.pypa.io/en/latest/userguide/"
"pyproject_config.html#setuptools-specific-configuration>`_, but there is no "
"alternative when ``man`` pages are a requirement."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:0
msgid "pdm-backend"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:180
msgid ""
"# pyproject.toml\n"
"[tool.pdm.build]\n"
"source-includes = [\"share\"]\n"
"\n"
"[tool.pdm.build.wheel-data]\n"
"data = [\n"
"  { path = \"share/man/man1/*\", relative-to = \".\" },\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:191
msgid ""
"The manual page ``manpage.1`` can then be read with ``man manpage`` after "
"installing the package. For a real-world example, see `pycowsay's setup.py "
"<https://github.com/cs01/pycowsay/blob/master/setup.py>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:195
msgid "Shell completions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:197
msgid ""
"A package can ship the completion script for its own command the same way it "
"ships a man page. pipx picks up the three directories a wheel installs "
"completion scripts into and links each one under ``PIPX_COMPLETION_DIR`` "
"(default ``~/.local/share``), so the completions arrive with the package and "
"leave with it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:205
msgid "Shipped by the package"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:206
msgid "Exposed to"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:207
msgid "Loaded by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:208
msgid "``share/bash-completion/completions/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:209
msgid "``~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:210
msgid "bash"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:211
msgid "``share/fish/vendor_completions.d/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:212
msgid "``~/.local/share/fish/vendor_completions.d/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:213
msgid "fish"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:214
msgid "``share/zsh/site-functions/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:215
msgid "``~/.local/share/zsh/site-functions/``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:216
msgid "zsh (with the directory on ``fpath``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:218
msgid ""
"Declare them through the data files of your build backend, exactly as with "
"man pages:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:220
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    name=\"your-tool\",\n"
"    data_files=[\n"
"        (\"share/bash-completion/completions\", [\"completions/your-"
"tool\"]),\n"
"        (\"share/zsh/site-functions\", [\"completions/_your-tool\"]),\n"
"        (\"share/fish/vendor_completions.d\", [\"completions/your-tool."
"fish\"]),\n"
"    ],\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:231
msgid ""
"See :doc:`../how-to/shell-completions` for how each shell loads these "
"directories."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:233
msgid ""
"You can read more about entry points in the `setuptools quickstart <https://"
"setuptools.pypa.io/en/latest/userguide/quickstart.html#entry-points-and-"
"automatic-script-creation>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/making-packages-compatible.rst:238
msgid ""
"This is often the ``[project]`` table, but it might be named differently. "
"Read more in the `PyPUG <https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/guides/"
"writing-pyproject-toml/#writing-your-pyproject-toml>`_."
msgstr ""
